<Window x:Class="ConditioningControlPanel.EmiBookWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="EMI"

        WindowStyle="None"
        AllowsTransparency="True"
        Background="Transparent"
        ShowActivated="False"
        ShowInTaskbar="False"
        ResizeMode="NoResize"
        SizeToContent="Manual"
        WindowStartupLocation="Manual"
        UseLayoutRounding="True"
        SnapsToDevicePixels="True"
        Topmost="True"
        Width="364" Height="728">

    <!--
        HER BOOK, the flipbook.

        A tall pixel panel that unfurls beside her, one feature per card, and every card is a little
        8-bit demo that SHOWS the feature happening with about thirty words of directions under it.
        It replaces the codex, which was a website in a window and which the owner rejected on
        sight: "imagine you are explaining this to people with ADHD".

        THE WINDOW RECIPE IS NOT OPTIONAL and is copied from her other three windows: WindowStyle
        None + AllowsTransparency + ShowActivated False + ShowInTaskbar False + Topmost, and
        WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW | WS_EX_NOACTIVATE stamped in OnSourceInitialized. She is a desktop
        ornament, not an application window.

        AllowsTransparency is also what makes the unfurl possible. The panel scales from a flat line
        to full size INSIDE a window that is already its final size, so the animation needs
        transparent air around it. It is also, separately, why this could never have been WebView2:
        a browser cannot paint in a layered window at all.

        WS_EX_NOACTIVATE means this window can never hold the keyboard, so everything in here is
        mouse-driven. There is nothing to type and nothing to tab to.

        NO Visibility="Hidden" HERE, and that is the one place this window must NOT copy her other
        three. Setting a Window's Visibility in XAML does not just set a property: WPF cannot touch
        an HWND that does not exist yet, so it QUEUES the hide as a dispatcher operation and applies
        it once the window is up. Her other panels are built at startup and opened much later, so
        that queued hide has long since drained by the time anybody calls Show. The book is built
        and opened in the SAME dispatcher turn, so the queued hide was still in flight and landed
        about 50 ms after Show: Window.ShowHelper called ShowWindow(SW_HIDE) on a window that had
        just been shown. WS_VISIBLE went off the HWND while Window.IsVisible and Window.Visibility
        both kept reading Visible, so nothing ever showed it again and every property WPF exposes
        insisted the panel was on screen.

        That is the whole of "i cant see the book, when i click the ? i just see emi say I read
        everything twice". The window has no reason to exist hidden - it is constructed only when
        it is about to be shown - so the property simply goes.

        EVERY BRUSH IS A LITERAL. This window has no resource dictionary behind it, and a
        StaticResource reaching for MainWindow's palette is the cross-dictionary BAML EndOfStream
        the Velvet Kit work was bitten by.

        FONTS ARE SET IN CODE, never by name here. Press Start 2P ships in Resources\emi\fonts and
        is loaded through a FontFamily base URI (EmiFace.PixelFont); naming it in XAML resolves
        against installed system fonts only, finds nothing, and silently falls back to the default
        UI face - the exact trap EmiRingWindow documents.

        GEOMETRY. 320 DIP wide: 2 of border each side leaves 316, less 12 of margin each side
        leaves 292, and the demo stage is 2 of border plus a 288 x 216 image. 288 is exactly three
        times the 96 x 72 buffer, so one buffer cell is nine screen pixels with no seam and no
        filtering. Change the width and that integer multiple is the first thing to re-derive.
    -->

    <Window.Resources>
        <!-- One tab chip. Lit chip is her pink with ink text; the rest are ink with a dull label. -->
        <Style x:Key="TabChip" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Focusable" Value="False"/>
            <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FF6B6890"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="Bd" Background="#FF0E0E1C" BorderBrush="#FF1C1C31" BorderThickness="0,0,2,0">
                            <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="Tag" Value="on">
                                <Setter TargetName="Bd" Property="Background" Value="#FFFF69B4"/>
                                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FF0E0E1C"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FF3A3856"/>
                                <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Arrow"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- The pager arrows, and the close x. -->
        <Style x:Key="ChromeChip" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Focusable" Value="False"/>
            <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FFF5F0E1"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="Bd" Background="#FF0E0E1C">
                            <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="Bd" Property="Background" Value="#FFFF69B4"/>
                                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FF0E0E1C"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FF3A3856"/>
                                <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Arrow"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!--
            ONE RAIL CHIP. The lit one wears her pink down its left edge and a raised ground; the
            rest are flat and their icon is dimmed in code. No text: a 44 pixel column has no room
            for a word, and the icon plus a tooltip is what the rail is for - somewhere to click AT
            rather than step towards.
        -->
        <Style x:Key="RailChip" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Focusable" Value="False"/>
            <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
            <Setter Property="Height" Value="40"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="Bd" Background="Transparent" BorderBrush="Transparent"
                                BorderThickness="3,0,0,0">
                            <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="Bd" Property="Background" Value="#FF2E2A45"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="Tag" Value="on">
                                <Setter TargetName="Bd" Property="Background" Value="#FF2E2A45"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="Bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="#FFFF69B4"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- TAKE ME THERE. Ghosted when the card has no detour, or when the door is locked. -->
        <Style x:Key="GoChip" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Focusable" Value="False"/>
            <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FF0E0E1C"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="Bd" Background="#FFFF69B4" BorderBrush="#FFA8386F" BorderThickness="0,0,0,3">
                            <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="Bd" Property="Background" Value="#FFFF87C4"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                                <Setter TargetName="Bd" Property="Background" Value="#FF34314F"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="Bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="#FF242239"/>
                                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FF7E7BA4"/>
                                <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Arrow"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>
    </Window.Resources>

    <Grid>
        <!--
            Two nested scale transforms, because the unfurl is two moves and they have different
            origins: a flat line shoots out FROM HER EDGE (ScaleX about the near edge), then it
            opens like a CRT coming on (ScaleY about the middle). One transform cannot do both.
            SweepHost's origin is flipped to the right edge in code when the panel sits on her left.
        -->
        <Border x:Name="SweepHost" RenderTransformOrigin="0,0.5">
            <Border.RenderTransform>
                <ScaleTransform x:Name="SweepScale" ScaleX="1" ScaleY="1"/>
            </Border.RenderTransform>

            <Grid x:Name="OpenHost" RenderTransformOrigin="0.5,0.5">
                <Grid.RenderTransform>
                    <ScaleTransform x:Name="OpenScale" ScaleX="1" ScaleY="1"/>
                </Grid.RenderTransform>

                <!--
                    THE SHADOW IS A HARD OFFSET BLOCK, not a DropShadowEffect.

                    A hard shadow is the right shadow for an 8-bit panel: the blurred one was the
                    only soft edge on the whole window, and it sat under a border drawn to land on
                    exact pixels. It is also cheaper. This window is LAYERED, which means WPF
                    composes it in software, and a 22 pixel gaussian over 320 x 668 was the most
                    expensive thing in the unfurl by a wide margin - for an effect nobody would
                    have described if asked what the book looks like.
                -->
                <Border Background="#B3000000" Margin="7,7,-7,-7" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>

                <Border x:Name="Panel"
                        Background="#FF252542"
                        BorderBrush="#FFFF69B4"
                        BorderThickness="2">

                    <Grid>
                    <DockPanel LastChildFill="True">

                    <!-- the three tab chips, and the way out -->
                    <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Top" Height="34" Background="#FF0E0E1C">
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="34"/>
                        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <Button x:Name="Tab0" Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource TabChip}"/>
                        <Button x:Name="Tab1" Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource TabChip}"/>
                        <Button x:Name="Tab2" Grid.Column="2" Style="{StaticResource TabChip}"/>
                        <Button x:Name="BtnClose" Grid.Column="3" Style="{StaticResource ChromeChip}"
                                Content="x"/>
                        <Border Grid.ColumnSpan="4" VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Height="2"
                                Background="#FF0E0E1C"/>
                    </Grid>

                    <!-- the pager -->
                    <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Height="44" Background="#FF1E1D34">
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="44"/>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="44"/>
                        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <Border Grid.ColumnSpan="3" VerticalAlignment="Top" Height="2" Background="#FF0E0E1C"/>
                        <Button x:Name="BtnPrev" Grid.Column="0" Margin="6,8,2,8"
                                Style="{StaticResource ChromeChip}" Content="&lt;"/>
                        <WrapPanel x:Name="Dots" Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal"
                                   HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        <Button x:Name="BtnNext" Grid.Column="2" Margin="2,8,6,8"
                                Style="{StaticResource ChromeChip}" Content="&gt;"/>
                    </Grid>

                    <!--
                        THE RAIL: one icon per card ON THIS TAB.

                        The book was four cards and a row of dots. At twenty-two the dots stopped
                        being navigation and became a progress bar - identical squares that say
                        where you are and nothing about where you could go, so reaching a card you
                        half remember meant flipping to it one page at a time.

                        Scoped to the tab on purpose. Twenty-two icons in this column would not fit
                        above the pager without shrinking them past reading size, and it would bury
                        the chapter breaks. The tab strip picks the chapter, the rail picks the page,
                        and the rail therefore never holds more than eight.

                        It is docked left BEFORE the card so the card keeps the whole remaining
                        width. The window grew by exactly this rail's 44, so on a desk with room the
                        text column is the same 290 it was measured at and no card reflows. The rail
                        keeps this width in the narrow book too - an icon column you have to squint
                        at is not navigation - so the narrow book pays for itself out of the card.
                    -->
                    <Border DockPanel.Dock="Left" Width="44" Background="#FF1E1D34"
                            BorderBrush="#FF0E0E1C" BorderThickness="0,0,2,0">
                        <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Hidden"
                                      HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                      PanningMode="VerticalOnly">
                            <StackPanel x:Name="Rail" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </ScrollViewer>
                    </Border>

                    <!--
                        THE CARD, and the row list is the whole layout argument.

                        The nudge row is the STRETCHY one and everything else is Auto, which is the
                        opposite of what it was: there used to be an air row between the bullets and
                        the button, so a short card left a hole in the middle and a long one had
                        nowhere to grow. Now the bullets take the slack (pinned to the top of their
                        row, so they still hug the gist) and the catch and the button sit against
                        the foot of the panel where the chrome belongs.

                        The catch has its own strip rather than a fifth bullet. The owner's ceiling
                        is four items per card, and the catch was spending one of them to say a
                        thing that is not an action - see EmiBookCards.
                    -->
                    <Grid Margin="12">
                        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>  <!-- the demo stage -->
                            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>  <!-- title          -->
                            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>  <!-- gist           -->
                            <RowDefinition Height="*"/>     <!-- the four nudges, and the slack -->
                            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>  <!-- the catch strip -->
                            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>  <!-- the one button  -->
                        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

                        <Border Grid.Row="0" x:Name="StageFrame" BorderBrush="#FF3B3962" BorderThickness="2"
                                Background="#FF0E0E1C" HorizontalAlignment="Left">
                            <Grid>
                                <Image x:Name="Stage" Width="288" Height="216"
                                       RenderOptions.BitmapScalingMode="NearestNeighbor"
                                       SnapsToDevicePixels="True"/>
                                <Border Background="#FF3B3962" HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                                        VerticalAlignment="Top" Padding="4,3">
                                    <TextBlock x:Name="StageLabel" Foreground="#FF0E0E1C"/>
                                </Border>
                            </Grid>
                        </Border>

                        <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" x:Name="CardTitle" Margin="0,12,0,0"
                                   Foreground="#FFF5F0E1" TextWrapping="Wrap" LineHeight="22"/>

                        <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" x:Name="CardGist" Margin="0,10,0,0"
                                   Foreground="#FFFF69B4" TextWrapping="Wrap" LineHeight="16"/>

                        <!--
                            The bullets scroll rather than clip. They never need to on a desktop
                            monitor - the longest card in the deck leaves about 30 pixels spare at
                            full height - but the panel shrinks to fit a short screen and the rows
                            around this one are all Auto, so an overflow would eat the bottom of
                            the last bullet with nothing to say it had. The bar is Hidden rather
                            than Auto because a scrollbar is the one piece of chrome on this window
                            that Windows would draw instead of us.

                            SO WE DRAW THE CUE OURSELVES. Hidden means the content still scrolls, it
                            just says nothing about it, and that was already a quiet way to lose the
                            end of a bullet on a short desk. The narrow book made it reachable: at a
                            196 wide column the densest card gains three lines, and the LOCKDOWN card
                            lost the last word of "then type let me out" - which is the one sentence
                            in the whole book somebody reads in a hurry. The chevron shows only when
                            there is something below, and it goes away once you have scrolled to it.
                        -->
                        <Grid Grid.Row="3" Margin="0,12,0,0">
                            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                                <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
                                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

                            <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="0" x:Name="NudgeScroll"
                                          VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Hidden"
                                          HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                          PanningMode="VerticalOnly"
                                          ScrollChanged="OnNudgeScrollChanged">
                                <StackPanel x:Name="Nudges" VerticalAlignment="Top"/>
                            </ScrollViewer>

                            <!--
                                ITS OWN BAND, not an overlay on the text. The first cut floated the
                                chevron over the bottom right of the copy, and on the card that
                                prompted all this it sat on the words: LOCKDOWN read "then type
                                let m [v]". A cue that hides the sentence it is pointing at is worse
                                than no cue, because the reader cannot even tell something is missing.

                                A row cannot oscillate. Showing it shrinks the viewport, which can
                                only ADD overflow, never remove it, so the cue never flickers itself
                                out of existence. It is inside the bullets' own star row, so the
                                catch strip and the button do not move when it appears.
                            -->
                            <Border Grid.Row="1" x:Name="MoreBelow" Visibility="Collapsed"
                                    IsHitTestVisible="False" HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                                    Margin="0,3,0,0" Padding="6,2">
                                <Polygon Points="0,0 10,0 5,6" Fill="#FFFF69B4"/>
                            </Border>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- The honest bit, in gold, with a rule down its left edge instead of a
                             bullet. Same fixed place on every card, so it is somewhere you can
                             look rather than something you have to find. -->
                        <Border Grid.Row="4" x:Name="CatchStrip" Margin="0,10,0,0"
                                Background="#FF2E2A45" BorderBrush="#FFF0C050"
                                BorderThickness="3,0,0,0" Padding="8,6,8,7">
                            <TextBlock x:Name="CardCatch" TextWrapping="Wrap" LineHeight="15"
                                       Foreground="#FFE8C46A"/>
                        </Border>

                        <Button Grid.Row="5" x:Name="BtnGo" Height="36" Margin="0,12,0,0"
                                Style="{StaticResource GoChip}"/>
                    </Grid>

                    </DockPanel>

                    <!-- The CRT flash on the unfurl. Never hit-testable, and INSIDE the panel
                         border: as a sibling of the panel it would paint over the transparent air
                         the unfurl scales through, which reads as a white rectangle with no panel
                         in it. -->
                    <Border x:Name="Flash" Background="#FFF5F0E1" Opacity="0" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                    </Grid>
                </Border>
            </Grid>
        </Border>
    </Grid>
</Window>
